<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Govan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Govan is a district on the south bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-clyde">River Clyde</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dumbarton-rock">Dumbarton Rock</a> in 870 it became a royal and religious centre of the kingdom of Strathclyde, and Govan Old Parish Church holds a collection of carved stones of the 9th to 11th centuries, including the Govan Sarcophagus and hogback stones. In modern times it was a major shipbuilding centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/govan.jpg" alt="Govan" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Allan · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Govan_ferry_-_geograph.org.uk_-_2981021.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Govan lies about 4 kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a> city centre, on the south bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-clyde">River Clyde</a>, opposite the mouth of the River Kelvin and the district of Partick. It is a district and parish and a former burgh, and it was historically part of the County of Lanark. A ford, and later a ferry, linked it with Partick for seasonal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> drovers. Its Scottish Gaelic name, Baile a' Ghobhainn, means the smith's town, and a place near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dumbarton-rock">Dumbarton Rock</a> called Ouania in the Historia regum Anglorum attributed to Symeon of Durham may be its earliest recorded name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Two Christian burials at Govan Old are radiocarbon dated to the 5th or 6th centuries, making Govan the earliest known Christian site in the region. The carved sandstone sarcophagus found in the churchyard in 1855 may have held the body or relics of St. Constantine, a Pictish king seemingly killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> in 877. In 1136, when Glasgow Cathedral was consecrated, King David I gave the church at Govan to the see, and it became a prebend of Glasgow. In 1454 a great flood brought down houses, barns and mills in the village.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">Coal</a> workings had developed around Craigton and Drumoyne by the 16th century, and dye works and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> mills followed. The deepening of the Clyde in 1759 and the building of quays and docks opened the way for shipbuilding: Robert Napier began building iron ships at Govan in 1841, and Randolph, Elder and Company, later the Fairfield Shipbuilding and Engineering Company, acquired land on the Fairfield estate in 1863 for a larger yard. Govan became a burgh in 1864, with Morris Pollok as its first provost, and its population rose from 9,000 then to 95,000 by 1907. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a> annexed it in 1912.</p><p>In 1971 the Upper Clyde Shipbuilders consortium, which included the Fairfield yard, went into receivership, and the unions answered with a work-in rather than a strike. The yard, renamed Govan Shipbuilders in 1973, was nationalised in 1977, sold to Kværner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> in 1988 and passed in 1999 to GEC's Marconi Marine and then to BAE Systems.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Govan Old Parish Church, rebuilt in 1884–1888, holds the Govan Stones, one of the finest collections of Early Christian stones in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. The district has been part of the City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a> since 1912 and is served by the Govan, Ibrox and Cessnock stations of the Glasgow subway.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Govan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 870 the royal centre moved to <b>Govan</b>, where a fine collection of carved stones survives.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-strathclyde">Kingdom of Strathclyde</a></li></ul></section>
